new file mode 100644
--- /dev/null
+++ b/Documentation/driver-api/thermal/linear.rst
@@ -0,0 +1,39 @@
+.. SPDX-License-Identifier: GPL-2.0-only
+
+Linear thermal governor
+=======================
+
+The ``linear`` governor interpolates active cooling states using ordinary
+thermal trips and cooling maps. This is a local governor, not an upstream
+Linux feature. Passive trips retain the step_wise governor's behavior. Hot
+and critical trip handling remains in thermal core.
+
+For an active map, the lower state is requested at its trip temperature. The
+upper state is reached at the next higher active trip in the same zone that
+is also bound to this cooling device. Between those temperatures the state
+is interpolated, with integer division rounding down. Beyond the endpoint it
+is clamped to the upper state. A terminal map requests its upper state as
+soon as it becomes active. A fixed-state map has equal lower and upper limits.
+Trip order does not matter. Equal-temperature trips do not form a segment.
+
+For a continuous curve, set each map's upper state equal to the next map's
+lower state, and give the last map equal lower and upper states. For example,
+a fan exposing PWM bytes as cooling states can use a 40 C active trip with
+limits 34..255 and an 80 C active trip with limits 255..255. This requests 34,
+89, 144, 199 and 255 at 40, 50, 60, 70 and 80 C respectively. Cooling states
+are driver-defined; the governor does not interpret them as RPM or duty.
+
+The core's hysteresis threshold determines whether a map is active. A 2 C
+hysteresis on the 40 C trip starts cooling at 40 C and keeps at least state
+34 until the temperature falls below 38 C. Use zero hysteresis on subsequent
+points to retrace the same line when cooling. Inactive maps release their
+request; the core applies the maximum demand across all maps and zones.
+
+Select ``CONFIG_THERMAL_DEFAULT_GOV_LINEAR`` for automatic boot-time control,
+or select ``linear`` through a zone's policy attribute. The zone must poll or
+otherwise deliver temperature updates throughout the curve; trip interrupts
+alone cannot continuously adjust the state within a segment. Hardware startup
+boosts belong in the fan driver. The governor re-applies active-device requests
+on each update so transient write failures are retried. Temperature read
+failures retain the last demand and use the thermal core's existing retry
+behavior; this governor does not introduce a separate sensor watchdog.
